 "steps": [
  {
   "id": "s0",
   "title": "What a heat engine does",
   "text": "A **heat engine** turns heat into useful work. It works between a **hot** reservoir (a furnace or flame) and a **cold** one (the air or a river). Every car, power station and steam engine is a heat engine.",
   "actions": [
    {
     "set": {
      "Qh": 100,
      "Qc": 60
     },
     "at": 0
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "s1",
   "title": "Heat in",
   "text": "The engine takes {{Qh|0}} J of heat \\( Q_h \\) from the hot reservoir. It uses this energy to push a piston or turn a turbine.",
   "actions": [
    {
     "set": {
      "Qh": 100,
      "Qc": 60
     },
     "at": 0
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "s2",
   "title": "Work out",
   "text": "Part of the heat becomes useful **work** \\( W \\) (the amber band). Here \\( W = {{W|0}} \\) J.",
   "actions": [
    {
     "set": {
      "Qh": 100,
      "Qc": 60
     },
     "at": 0
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "s3",
   "title": "Heat must be rejected",
   "text": "The rest, \\( Q_c = {{Qc|0}} \\) J, has to be thrown away into the cold reservoir. By energy conservation \\( Q_h = W + Q_c \\). The engine cannot keep all the heat as work.",
   "actions": [
    {
     "set": {
      "Qh": 100,
      "Qc": 60
     },
     "at": 0
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "s4",
   "title": "Efficiency",
   "text": "The **efficiency** is the fraction of the heat taken in that becomes work: \\( \\eta = W/Q_h = {{eta|0}}\\% \\). Reduce the rejected heat and the efficiency rises. Can it ever reach 100%? (See the second law.)",
   "actions": [
    {
     "set": {
      "Qh": 100,
      "Qc": 60
     },
     "at": 0
    },
    {
     "tween": {
      "Qc": 30
     },
     "at": 0,
     "dur": 3600
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
 "readouts": [
